INT. CONVERTED CATHEDRAL CURLING HALL - NIGHT

A deconsecrated church, nave stripped, pews gone, a pebbled sheet of ice running where the altar once stood. Stained glass throws blue and amber across the frost. Stone saints watch from niches. Condensation drips from the vaulted ceiling.

Scoreboard: LIONS 6, FERRYMEN 6. Eighth end.

BERNADETTE "BERNIE" OYELARAN (50s, coach's jacket, whistle she never uses) stands behind the hog line with a clipboard she hasn't looked at in an hour.

MAEVE KOWALCZYK (20s, skip, braid tucked into her collar) crouches at the house, broom flat across her knees.

DESMOND TAI (30s, lead, calves like fence posts) waits at the far hack, stone handle in a white-knuckled grip.

RAFFI HOVSEPIAN (20s, second, perpetual grin gone tight) sweeps nothing, just to have something to do with his arms.

JUNE PELLETIER-ODUYA (40s, vice, wedding ring on a chain around her neck) stands at the boards, arms folded, chewing a toothpick down to splinters.

                    BERNIE
          Last stone. Hammer's ours.

                    MAEVE
          I know.

                    BERNIE
          Draw to the button.

                    MAEVE
          I know.

                    BERNIE
          Then why are you looking at the saint?

Maeve glances at a stone St. Sebastian in the wall, pierced with nine marble arrows.

                    MAEVE
          He's got a better angle than I do.

                    DESMOND
          (calling down the sheet)
          Ready!

                    JUNE
          Ice is pulling left, maybe two inches.

                    RAFFI
          Three. It's three.

                    JUNE
          Two and a half.

                    RAFFI
          Fine. Two and a half.

Desmond pushes out of the hack, glides low and long, releases the stone with a soft rotation. It slides, humming on the pebble.

                    MAEVE
          Hard! Hard!

Raffi and June sweep, brooms drumming the ice, snow-frost jumping off the bristles. The stone arcs past the guard.

                    JUNE
          Off!

They lift. The stone curls, slows, rests an inch from the back of the four-foot ring.

                    BERNIE
          That's yours, Desmond.

                    DESMOND
          I know.

                    BERNIE
          Everyone says "I know" tonight. I'd like one "I'm scared."

A beat. Raffi laughs. June spits her toothpick onto the ice. Maeve rises, rolling her shoulders.

                    MAEVE
          I'm scared.

                    BERNIE
          Good. Go.

Maeve settles into the hack. Looks down the sheet. Breathes.

                    MAEVE (CONT'D)
          Desmond, where do you want it?

                    DESMOND
          Weight of a feather. Land on the button like a kiss.

                    MAEVE
          Don't kiss anything, I'm married.

                    JUNE
          So am I.

                    RAFFI
          Everybody's married. Throw the rock.

Maeve pushes. The stone leaves her hand in a perfect curve. Desmond and Raffi pick up the sweep, then drop it. June holds up a flat palm.

                    JUNE
          Leave it. Leave it.

The stone drifts, rotating, down the pebbled ice, under a rose window that paints it red for one second, then amber, then blue.

                    BERNIE
          (whispering)
          Come on, come on.

It stops. A hair's width from Desmond's stone, a hair's width from the pin.

Silence under the vault. Raffi drops his broom. Desmond puts both hands on his head. June exhales through her teeth.

                    JUNE (CONT'D)
          We measure.

Bernie gets down on her knees on the ice, pulls a measuring stick from her jacket, and lays it across the gap between the two stones. She reads it. Looks up.

                    BERNIE
          Ferrymen's. By a quarter inch.

Nobody speaks. Maeve takes off her glove, looks at her bare hand. Her fingers don't tremble.

                    MAEVE
          Good end.

                    BERNIE
          That's the end. Not the match. We play the extra end in five.

                    DESMOND
          There's an extra end?

                    BERNIE
          There's always an extra end. Sit down. Drink water. Look at a saint.
